"EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16523)\nciteturn107academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 days ago; Title: EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\nTitle: EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\nAuthors: Min Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\nDate: Mon Oct 12 10:11:38 2026\n\nTraining LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.16523] EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents (https://borowiecki.dev/abs/2610.16523)\nciteturn107search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; # Title:EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents ... View a PDF of the paper titled EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents, by Min Cai and Dan Zhang and Gao Xing and Libin Ma and Zhaohui Xu and Xi Ye\n\n# Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\n\n[Submitted on 12 Oct 2026]\n\n# Title:EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\nAuthors:Min Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents, by Min Cai and Dan Zhang and Gao Xing and Libin Ma and Zhaohui Xu and Xi Ye\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:Training LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.\n\nSubjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2610.16523 [cs.AI]\n   | (or arXiv:2610.16523v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.16523 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration)\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Min Cai [view email]\n[v1] Mon, 12 Oct 2026 10:11:38 UTC (1,774 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents | Arxiv - DeepPaper (https://jp.ibbac.eu.org/papers/2610.16523v1)\nciteturn107search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: yesterday; EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\n  1.   2. Paper List\n  3. EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\n# EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\nPDF DeepRead\n\n### Key Contributions\n\nIntroduces a meta-agent framework (EAU) that dynamically adapts environment, user simulator, and reward design during training, leading to significant performance gains in multi-turn agent tasks.\n\n### Abstract\n\nTraining LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.\n\n### Summary\n\nEAU is a meta-agent framework that enables the co-evolution of environment, user simulator, and agent policy to improve multi-turn RL training stability and performance.\n\n### Subjects\n\ncs.AI cs.CL cs.LG\n\n### arXiv\n\nImage: arXiv 2610.16523v1\n\n### Paper Type\n\nframework Method\n\n### Domain\n\nReinforcement Learning LLM Agent\n\n